            <ArticleTitle>Should Broca's area include Brodmann area 47?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>73-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7334/psicothema2016.11</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND">Understanding brain organization of speech production has been a principal goal of neuroscience. Historically, brain speech production has been associated with so-called Broca’s area (Brodmann area –BA- 44 and 45), however, modern neuroimaging developments suggest speech production is associated with networks rather than with areas. The purpose of this paper was to analyze the connectivity of BA47 ( pars orbitalis) in relation to language .</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD">A meta-analysis was conducted to assess the language network in which BA47 is involved. The Brainmap database was used. Twenty papers corresponding to 29 experimental conditions with a total of 373 subjects were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">Our results suggest that BA47 participates in a “frontal language production system” (or extended Broca’s system). The BA47  connectivity found is also concordant with a minor role in language semantics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS">BA47 plays a central role in the language production system.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
